#### {{ page.metadata.description }}

<h4 class="release-note release-note__new">NEW</h4>

* **String literals accept backslash escapes — and a literal backslash must now be doubled** (2026-08-20): A backslash inside a Liquid string literal escapes the character after it, so the delimiter can finally appear inside a literal of the same kind, and `\\` is one literal backslash:

{% raw %}
```liquid
{{ "say \"hi\"" }}          => say "hi"
{{ 'it\'s here' }}          => it's here
{{ 'a\\b' }}                => a\b
```
{% endraw %}

  Only the two delimiters and the backslash itself are escape targets. Any other `\X` stays exactly as written, so patterns and paths are unaffected — `'\d+'`, `'\.'`, `'\['` and `'C:\temp'` all mean what they always did. Previously a literal ended at the first quote character whatever preceded it, so {% raw %}`{{ "say \"hi\"" }}`{% endraw %} compared and printed the wrong value while the rest of the markup was silently dropped. The rule now applies everywhere markup is parsed: {% raw %}`{% assign %}`{% endraw %}, filter arguments, {% raw %}`{% if %}`{% endraw %} / {% raw %}`{% unless %}`{% endraw %} / {% raw %}`{% case %}`{% endraw %} conditions, {% raw %}`{% render %}`{% endraw %} / {% raw %}`{% include %}`{% endraw %} / {% raw %}`{% function %}`{% endraw %} attributes, {% raw %}`{% cycle %}`{% endraw %} values and {% raw %}`{% for %}`{% endraw %} / {% raw %}`{% tablerow %}`{% endraw %} sources. See [Types](/api-reference/liquid/types).

  **This is a breaking change for one spelling.** {% raw %}`'\'`{% endraw %} and {% raw %}`"\"`{% endraw %} are no longer one-backslash strings — the backslash escapes the closing quote, so the literal never terminates. Code that used that spelling to build regex-safe or URL-escaped values has to be rewritten with the doubled form:

{% raw %}
```liquid
{% assign a = value | remove: '\' %}              {% comment %} before {% endcomment %}
{% assign a = value | remove: '\\' %}            {% comment %} after  {% endcomment %}

{% assign b = value | replace: "\", "%5C" %}     {% comment %} before {% endcomment %}
{% assign b = value | replace: "\\", "%5C" %}    {% comment %} after  {% endcomment %}
```
{% endraw %}

  Most occurrences fail loudly: the file is rejected at deploy with `Liquid syntax error (line N): Unexpected character ...`, so a deploy tells you where they are. A literal that contains `\\`, `\"` or `\'` parses either way and changes value instead, so search your code for those three sequences before deploying.

* **`escape_regex` filter** (2026-08-22): Escapes every character that has a special meaning in a regular expression, so the result matches the input literally. Use it on any value that goes into a pattern for [replace_regex](/api-reference/liquid/platformos-filters#replace_regex), [matches](/api-reference/liquid/platformos-filters#matches) or [regex_matches](/api-reference/liquid/platformos-filters#regex_matches) — without it a value carrying `(`, `|` or `\` changes the structure of the pattern instead of being matched by it, and escaping by hand with chained {% raw %}`replace:`{% endraw %} calls is bypassable:

{% raw %}
```liquid
{{ 'Jane.Doe' | escape_regex }} => Jane\.Doe

{% liquid
  assign safe = mention | escape_regex
  assign pattern = '@\[' | append: safe | append: '\]'
  assign output = body | replace_regex: pattern, link
%}
```
{% endraw %}

<h4 class="release-note release-note__improved">IMPROVED</h4>

* **Liquid errors reported at deploy name the line they are on** (2026-08-22): A syntax error, an unknown tag or an unknown filter in a file being deployed is now reported as `Liquid syntax error (line 4): Unknown filters: not_a_filter`.

* **Every alias is published as a deprecated spelling of its canonical filter** (2026-08-14): The [filter reference](/api-reference/liquid/platformos-filters) used to list an alias only inside the canonical filter's `aliases` array, which is not where a consumer looks: [platformos-check](/developer-guide/platformos-check/checks/deprecated-filter)'s deprecation check matches a filter's **name**, so {% raw %}`{{ x | parse_json }}`{% endraw %} drew a warning while {% raw %}`{{ x | to_hash }}`{% endraw %} — the same filter — drew none. Each alias is now an entry of its own, marked deprecated, carrying the canonical filter's own arity and parameters so no working call is refused, and naming a successor that is not itself deprecated. Expect your editor to start flagging spellings it used to accept in silence, among them `compact`, `select`, `reject`, `detect`, `any`, `sort_by`, `group_by`, `flatten`, `dig`, `fetch`, `to_json`, `markdownify`, `nl2br`, `translate`, `localize` and `to_hash`. **Every one of them keeps working** — this is published metadata and an autofix target (`pos-cli check run -a`), not a removal.

  `t`, `t_escape` and `l` are the canonical spellings of the translation and localization filters, and `translate`, `translate_escape` and `localize` are the deprecated aliases. Their error messages still say `translate filter - ...`: they name what the filter does, and templates assert on them.

* filter and tag arguments carry the information a consumer needs to act on them: the declared type of each argument and of the return value (`string`, `number`, `boolean`, `object`, `array`, `string[]`, a drop name, …), whether an argument is required, whether it is passed positionally or as `name: value`, and how many arguments the filter accepts. Tag arguments used to publish `untyped` for all of them regardless of what the platform declared.

* **`parse_json` filter is deprecated** (2026-08-15): {% raw %}`{% assign %}`{% endraw %} [takes a JSON literal directly](/api-reference/liquid/variables) and produces the same Hash with no string in between. Note this is a rewrite rather than a rename — the quoted JSON becomes markup, and a value that was interpolated into the string with the `json` filter becomes a plain expression:

{% raw %}
```liquid
{% assign object = '{ "name": "foo", "bar": {} }' | parse_json %}
{% assign object = { "name": "foo", "bar": {} } %}

{% assign name = "foo" %}
{% assign object = '{ "name": {{ name | json }} }' | parse_json %}
{% assign object = { "name": name } %}
```
{% endraw %}

  The rewrite covers JSON written in the **template**, which is what the filter is mostly used for. A JSON document that arrives at **runtime** — an `api_call` response body, `download_file` output — reaches Liquid as a string, and {% raw %}`{% assign %}`{% endraw %} stores exactly the string it was given, so `parse_json` remains the only step that turns one into a Hash and is not going anywhere for that use.

* **Ruby 4** (2026-08-12): The platform now runs on Ruby 4.0.

* **Deploy reports the state of the asset phase** (2026-08-18): Assets are uploaded and registered after the archive phase has already been polled to completion, so a release row said `success` while its assets were still being processed and a client could not tell "not finished yet" from "this server does not report on assets". A release now carries `asset_status` — `in_progress` from the moment its asset manifest is accepted, then `success` or `error` — and the deploy report, the asset status and the asset report each merge into the stored report instead of overwriting one another.

<h4 class="release-note release-note__fixed">FIXED</h4>

* **{% raw %}`{% return %}`{% endraw %} and {% raw %}`{% redirect_to %}`{% endraw %} work inside {% raw %}`{% for %}`{% endraw %} and {% raw %}`{% tablerow %}`{% endraw %} loops** (2026-08-19): A {% raw %}`{% return %}`{% endraw %} placed inside a loop was ignored — the loop ran to completion and the interrupt never reached the {% raw %}`{% function %}`{% endraw %} tag waiting for it. A partial with a fallback {% raw %}`{% return %}`{% endraw %} after the loop silently returned that fallback instead, so a permission-granting partial that returned `true` from inside a loop always fell through to its closing `return false` and every grant it was responsible for quietly stopped working. With no fallback, the function tag raised `function X must return a value via a {% raw %}{% return %}{% endraw %} tag` instead. {% raw %}`{% redirect_to %}`{% endraw %} was swallowed the same way and kept rendering the rest of the template after the redirect.

  A {% raw %}`{% return %}`{% endraw %} now returns the value of the iteration it ran in, stops the loop, and propagates out of nested loops and out of {% raw %}`{% tablerow %}`{% endraw %}; {% raw %}`{% redirect_to %}`{% endraw %} stops the loop and the rest of the template. {% raw %}`{% break %}`{% endraw %} and {% raw %}`{% continue %}`{% endraw %} are unchanged, byte for byte. A {% raw %}`{% return %}`{% endraw %} inside an {% raw %}`{% include %}`{% endraw %}d partial still goes nowhere, which is by design — {% raw %}`{% function %}`{% endraw %} is what collects a returned value.

* **A `%}` written in a comment no longer truncates a {% raw %}`{% liquid %}`{% endraw %} tag** (2026-08-06): Inside {% raw %}`{% liquid %}`{% endraw %} a line starting with `#` is a comment that runs to the end of that line, but the tokenizer closed the tag at the first `%}` it found anywhere — including one inside such a comment. Every directive after that comment leaked out as page text:

{% raw %}
```liquid
{% liquid
  # a comment mentioning %} the rest
  assign d = 21 | times: 2
%}R=[{{ d }}]
```
{% endraw %}

  rendered {% raw %}` the rest\n  assign d = 21 | times: 2\n%}R=[]`{% endraw %} instead of `R=[42]`. A comment is a comment: `%}`, {% raw %}`}}`{% endraw %}, {% raw %}`{%`{% endraw %} and anything else after a `#` is comment content wherever on the line it sits, and only a `%}` outside a comment closes the tag. A tag whose every `%}` is commented out is reported as unterminated — {% raw %}`Tag '{%' was not properly terminated`{% endraw %} — exactly like a tag written with no `%}` at all, rather than being silently truncated.

* **Asset URLs containing percent-encoded characters** (2026-08-12): An asset path was taken from the normalized, percent-decoded request path and passed upstream without being re-escaped, stripping one level of encoding — `%20` reached storage as a raw space and broke the request. The still-encoded path is now forwarded, so assets whose names contain spaces or other escaped characters load.

* **A killed background job no longer blocks every later run of the same job** (2026-08-14): Deploys, exports, imports, instance clones and Elasticsearch reindexing take a uniqueness lock so two of them cannot run at once, and a job that conflicts with a held lock reschedules itself five seconds later. The reaper that clears the locks of dead jobs was never started and the death handler was never registered, so a job killed mid-run (an OOM, a hard restart) left its lock behind and the next request for that work requeued itself every five seconds for as long as the stale lock lived — up to a day, with nothing in the queue looking wrong. The reaper now runs, is resurrected if the process holding it dies, and the lock lifetime is capped at an hour.

* **A request body that cannot be parsed answers 400 or 501, and is logged** (2026-08-11): An unparseable request body was answered with `415 Unsupported Media Type` whatever the cause, and nothing was recorded, so there was no way to tell a malformed payload from a body the platform received still wrapped in a transfer coding it never got to decode — which is what happens when an intermediary terminating HTTP/2 strips a `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` header without decoding the body. The response is now `501` when an undecoded transfer coding reaches the application and `400` for a body that is simply malformed, the message says which of the two it was and what to do about it, and the failure is logged with the URL, the content type, the declared length and the parser class — never the body itself.
